  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b6c94dd4c19312240817d1d9adc28902, type: 3}
  m_Name: GandharaBuddha
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ARMuseum.Exhibits.ExhibitData
  title: GANDHARA BUDDHA
  subtitle: Buddha image in the Gandharan style, c. 3rd century CE
  category: "Indian Heritage \u2022 Gandharan Sculpture"
  description: A Buddha image in the Gandharan style, from the ancient region of Gandhara in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent, now largely in Pakistan and Afghanistan. Gandharan sculptors combined Buddhist devotion with the naturalistic figure carving and drapery of Hellenistic art, which reached the region through trade and cultural contact after Alexander's campaigns.
  symbolism:
  - label: Urna
    meaning: In Buddhist tradition, one of the 32 major marks of a Buddha, often associated with insight.
  - label: Ushnisha
    meaning: A cranial protuberance on the crown, often associated with wisdom and enlightenment.
  - label: Hand gestures (mudra)
    meaning: Hand positions in Buddhist images are used to convey teaching, reassurance or meditation. The specific gesture shown in this model is not identified in this exhibit.
  - label: Drapery
    meaning: The clinging, folded robe is often linked to Hellenistic influence on Gandharan sculpture.
  historicalContext: Gandhara was a major centre of Buddhism under the Kushan Empire, roughly the 1st to 3rd centuries CE, with monasteries, stupas and workshops producing Buddha and bodhisattva images in stone and stucco. The 3rd century CE falls within this flourishing period. Gandharan workshops are among the most influential early centres for depicting the Buddha in human form.
  provenanceNote: Museum attribution, findspot and acquisition details are not yet confirmed for this exhibit. The 3D model and textures come from the supplied source files.
  narration: This is the Gandhara Buddha. Gandharan sculptors, working in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent around the third century of the common era, combined Buddhist devotion with the naturalistic drapery and figure carving of Hellenistic art. Look at the folds of the robe, the hair and the features, and the marks associated with the Buddha, such as the urna between the brows and the ushnisha on the crown.
  narrationClip: {fileID: 0}
  exhibitId: gandhara-buddha
  thumbnail: {fileID: 0}
  artifactPrefab: {fileID: 192544251322971070, guid: fb45dc8fe0f847c40bb9f84c49753b45, type: 3}
  displayHeightMeters: 0.3
  period: c. 3rd century CE, Gandharan style
  introduction: A Buddha image in the Gandharan style, from the ancient region of Gandhara in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent, now largely in Pakistan and Afghanistan.
  culturalSignificance: Gandharan sculptors combined Buddhist devotion with naturalistic figure carving and drapery influenced by Hellenistic art, which reached the region through trade and cultural contact after Alexander's campaigns. Gandharan images are widely regarded as among the early influential depictions of the Buddha in human form, and their styles spread along trade routes across Asia.
  detailImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 499d7b2208fd17543b384db9f1d92dd4, type: 3}
